// @check
// @error no `other` on `T` — `Showable` is not a bound
// @error `Showable` is not a bound — a bound is a type with method members (`type Showable = { m(): R }`) or an inline `{ m(): R }`
// @error no `other` on `T` — its bound `{show:()=>string}` grants `show`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `s` is inferred as `{show: () => string}` — remove it to lean on inference
// THE SUB-CASE THAT MADE THE OLD SENTENCE FALSE, AND THE ONE IT HAD TO BE TOLD APART FROM.
// `Showable` HAS a member — `show` — and is still not a bound, because a NAMED bound is a
// type declaring METHOD members (OQ-1's call shape) and this one declares a field. So "its
// bound `Showable` is not a type with members" was a false statement about the author's own
// declaration, which is why the member floor now says only that the annotation is not a bound.
//
// `e` IS THE SAME DEMAND SPELLED INLINE, AND IT REACHES THE OTHER RUNG. An inline `{ … }` is
// an object spelling rather than a name lookup, so it is a bound even with a field member,
// and its refusal names the bound and everything it grants. Two sentences, both true, both
// here — a narrowing that merged them would redden this file rather than pass it.
type Showable = { show: () => string }

function d<T: Showable>(x: T): string {
  x.other()
}

function e<T: { show: () => string }>(x: T): string {
  x.other()
}

const s: Showable = { show: () => "s" }
print(d(s))
print(e(s))
